#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-01.png", width: 466.5pt), 64.5pt, 224pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 17.041pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(97.68pt, 100.92pt, "Data-Driven Automated Identification of Optimal Feature-")
  #line-at(71.52pt, 120.48pt, "Representative Images in Infrared Thermography Using Statistical ")
  #line-at(202.44pt, 140.04pt, "and Morphological Metrics. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.799pt, 170.402pt, "Harutyun Yagdjian")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.209pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(167.641pt, 165.841pt, "a")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(171.36pt, 170.402pt, ", Martin Gurka")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.209pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(246.604pt, 165.841pt, "a ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 194.16pt, "a")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(74.28pt, 197.64pt, "Leibniz-Institut für Verbundwerkstoffe GmbH, Erwin-Schrödinger-Straße 58, 67663 Kaiserslautern ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 243.72pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.799pt, 265.077pt, "Infrared thermography (IRT) is a widely used non-destructive testing technique for ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 278.882pt, "detecting structural features, such as subsurface. Most IRT data post-processing ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 292.681pt, "methodologies result in the generation of image sequences in which defect visibility varies ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 306.48pt, "strongly across time, frequency, or coefficient/index domains, making the selection of ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 320.278pt, "defect-representative images a non-trivial and critical task. Conventional evaluation ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 334.077pt, "metrics such as the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) or the Tanimoto criterion often rely on prior ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 347.882pt, "knowledge of defect location or defect free reference regions, which limits their ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 361.681pt, "applicability for automated and unsupervised analysis.")
  #line-at(373.677pt, 361.681pt, "In this work, a data-driven ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 375.479pt, "methodology is proposed to identify images within IRT datasets that are most likely to ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 389.278pt, "contain and represent features, especially anomalies and defects, without requiring any ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 403.076pt, "prior information about their spatial position. The investigation focuses on three ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 416.882pt, "complementary metrics. Firstly the Homogeneity Index of Mixture (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.268pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.644pt, 416.882pt, "HI")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(429.842pt, 416.882pt, "), which quantifies ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 430.681pt, "statistical heterogeneity through deviations of local intensity distributions from a global ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 444.48pt, "reference distribution. The second metric is a Representative Elementary Area (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.268pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(497.04pt, 444.48pt, "REA")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(520.075pt, 444.48pt, ") ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 458.279pt, "derived from a Minkowski-functional-based adaptation of the Representative Elementary ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 472.078pt, "Volume (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.268pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(120.36pt, 472.078pt, "REV")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(143.397pt, 472.078pt, ") concept to two-dimensional images. Building upon these approaches, a ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 485.88pt, "third metric is introduced: a geometrical-topological Total Variation Energy (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.268pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(466.083pt, 485.88pt, "TVE")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.283pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(488.398pt, 485.88pt, ") index ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 499.679pt, "based on two-dimensional Minkowski functionals, designed to enhance sensitivity to ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 513.478pt, "localized anomalies. The proposed framework is validated experimentally using pulse-")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 527.276pt, "heated IRT data acquired from a carbon fiber-reinforced polymer (CFRP) plate containing ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 541.075pt, "six artificial defects at depths between 0.135 mm and 0.810 mm and is further supported ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 554.881pt, "by one-dimensional N-layer thermal model (NLM) simulations. The results demonstrate ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 568.68pt, "that the proposed metrics enable robust, unbiased ranking of image sequences and provide ")
  #line-at(70.799pt, 582.479pt, "a reliable basis for automated defect-oriented image selection in IRT. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 602.16pt, "Keywords: ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(126.24pt, 602.16pt, "composite materials, non-destructive testing (NDT), infrared thermography (IRT), ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 615.96pt, "representative elementary volume (REV), image analysis, homogeneity index (HI)  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 667.2pt, "1.")
  #line-at(88.8pt, 667.2pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 692.76pt, "The reliable and efficient non-destructive testing (NDT) of composite materials is of increasing ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 706.56pt, "importance, as material defects can critically affect the structural integrity of components. In ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 720.36pt, "this context, infrared thermography (IRT) provides a rapid, non-contact approach for detecting ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 734.16pt, "thermal anomalies potentially indicative of hidden defects. A range of post-processing ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 747.96pt, "methodologies has been developed to enhance the contrast of defect signals from thermograms, ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 761.76pt, "including, but not limited to, the following: Pulse Phase Thermography (PPT) [1], ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 775.56pt, "Thermographic Signal Reconstruction (TSR) [2], Principal Component Thermography (PCT) ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 81.72pt, "[3], ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(92.04pt, 81.72pt, "Differential Absolute Contrast (DAC) and its modifications (MDAC) [4-6], Thermal Shock ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 95.04pt, "Response Spectrum (TSRS) [7-9], and many more.")
  #line-at(307.68pt, 95.04pt, "The IRT experiment itself, like, generates large ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 108.36pt, "amounts of data. During an IRT experiment, usually a time series of thermograms is recorded for ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 121.56pt, "each sample. In PPT, the data is then transformed from the time- into the frequency domain using ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 134.76pt, "a discrete Fourier transform (DFT). This process yields amplitude and phase spectra, whereby a ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 147.96pt, "characteristic penetration depth can be interpreted based on its corresponding frequency.")
  #line-at(487.44pt, 147.96pt, "In TSR, ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 161.16pt, "the cooling curves of the thermograms – excluding the saturation phase [10] – are fitted pixel-wise ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 174.96pt, "with an ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 11.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(108.72pt, 174.96pt, "𝑛")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.52pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(115.56pt, 174.96pt, "-th degree polynomial, and only the resulting coefficients are stored. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(437.64pt, 174.96pt, "Despite the above ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 188.76pt, "mentioned differences in the specific processing approaches, the examples illustrate a general ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 202.56pt, "principle: a significant number of IRT post-processing methods, whether explicitly or ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 216.36pt, "implicitly, generate structurally similar datasets in the form of images. They are typically ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 230.16pt, "represented as a three-dimensional image sequence, either with time, frequency, or a series of ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 243.96pt, "coefficients as the third dimension. These can then subsequently be analyzed to detect and ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 257.76pt, "characterize defects in the examined specimen. Figure 1 schematically illustrates this general ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 271.56pt, "situation. In practice, defect analysis leads to a key challenge: namely, determining which image ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 285.36pt, "within the sequence provides the most reliable representation of the defect, i.e., exhibits the ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 299.16pt, "highest defect contrast. The evaluation of the effectivity of different IRT data evaluation ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 312.84pt, "methods is typically carried out using specimens with integrated, well-defined artificial defects, ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 326.64pt, "for which parameters such as size, position, depth, and material are known. In this context, ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 340.44pt, "quantitative assessment and comparison are often based on metrics such as the signal-to-noise ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 354.24pt, "ratio (SNR) [11] and the Tanimoto criterion (TC) [12]. The Tanimoto criterion is a ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 368.04pt, "dimensionless parameter that enables a fast and objective comparison of images or other data. ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 381.84pt, "It involves information about correctly and incorrectly identified, as well as undetected, defect ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 395.64pt, "pixels. Owing to this property, it can be effectively used to assess the performance of different ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 409.44pt, "signal post-processing techniques and to compare them with one another regarding their ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 423.24pt, "capability to detect hidden defects in the depicted specimen. However, the accuracy of the ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 437.04pt, "Tanimoto criterion depends critically on the quality of the defect detection or contour search ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 450.84pt, "algorithm, since even minor inaccurately detected boundaries of defects can significantly affect ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 464.64pt, "the calculated result. The SNR is defined in various ways depending on the scientific discipline ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 478.44pt, "and the practical context of its application [13-15]. In infrared thermography, it provides a ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 492.24pt, "measure of the contrast between the defective area (region of interest) and a corresponding ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 506.04pt, "reference region. It is noteworthy that the measured SNR can be influenced by factors such as ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 519.84pt, "lateral heat flow, uneven heating, and also by the selection of the reference region. These factors ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 533.64pt, "may affect the absolute SNR values and the quantitative assessment of defect contrast. ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 547.44pt, "However, metrics that rely on the known position of a defect or on a carefully selected reference ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 561.24pt, "region are often of limited practical use, particularly in real-world scenarios and automated data ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 575.04pt, "processing, when the position of a potential defect is not known. In this paper, therefore, we ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 588.84pt, "propose a methodology that enables the identification of images within a dataset that are most ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 602.64pt, "likely to contain and represent a defect. This approach is of special relevance in scenarios where ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 616.44pt, "the existence of a defect is unknown, as it enables an unbiased, data-driven selection of images ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 630.24pt, "with the greatest likelihood of revealing anomalies. The approach provides a robust framework ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 644.04pt, "for automated processing and reliable decision-making in infrared thermography datasets and ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 657.84pt, "beyond by decoupling the analysis from prior knowledge of defect location or reference ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 671.64pt, "selection. Moreover, a comparison and evaluation of the empirical results with the theoretical ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 685.44pt, "results is provided, based on one-dimensional thermal N-layer model (NLM) simulations [16]. ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 699.24pt, "For our purposes, we use three different metrics: the first is the Homogeneity Index of Mixture ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 713.04pt, "(")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(74.76pt, 713.04pt, "HI")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(87.36pt, 713.04pt, "), which quantifies statistical heterogeneity by measuring deviations of local intensity ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 726.84pt, "distributions from the global reference distribution. Conceptually, this measure is related to ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 740.64pt, "histogram-based homogeneity measures and distributional criteria used in image analysis [17-")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 754.44pt, "19] as well as to classical mixing indices from mixture theory, where spatial uniformity is ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 768.24pt, "described through distributional metrics [20-22].The second metric used is derived from a ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 782.04pt, "widely used methodology called Representative Elementary Volume (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(411.24pt, 782.04pt, "REV")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(433.32pt, 782.04pt, ") analysis [23, 24]. ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 442.5pt), 72.5pt, 261pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 81.72pt, "More specifically, it is based on a variant of this methodology that uses morphological measures ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 95.52pt, "on porous media - i.e., the Minkowski functional - to evaluate the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(400.32pt, 95.52pt, "REV")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(422.4pt, 95.52pt, " [25]. In general, the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 109.32pt, "REV")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(92.76pt, 109.32pt, " analysis method identifies the smallest region of a heterogeneous material that sufficiently ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 123.12pt, "represents its overall geometrical and physical properties, enabling the estimation of ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 136.92pt, "macroscopic behavior from a limited sample. We adapt the methodology to describe two-")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 150.72pt, "dimensional images using a numerical metric called Representative Elementary Area (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(495.36pt, 150.72pt, "REA")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(517.44pt, 150.72pt, "). ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 164.52pt, "Building upon these approaches, we introduce a third metric that is specifically designed to ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 178.32pt, "respond more sensitively to anomalies within image sequences, as will be demonstrated in the ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 192.12pt, "following sections. This metric is a consolidated index based on two-dimensional Minkowski ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 205.92pt, "functional, referred to as the geometrical-topological Total Variation Energy (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(446.28pt, 205.92pt, "TVE")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(467.64pt, 205.92pt, ") index. For ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 219.72pt, "this purpose, a CFRP plate containing six artificial defects integrated at varying depths (from ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 233.52pt, "0.135 mm to 0.810 mm) was employed as the test specimen. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 614.04pt, "2.")
  #line-at(88.8pt, 614.04pt, "Theory ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 632.88pt, "2.1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.322pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(106.801pt, 632.88pt, "Pulse Phase Thermography (PPT) ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 658.44pt, "The development of Pulse Phase Thermography (PPT) was driven by the objective of ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 672.24pt, "integrating the strengths of both pulsed (IT) and modulated (LIT) infrared thermography. This ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 686.04pt, "integration involved isolating the various frequencies produced in pulsed infrared ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 699.84pt, "thermography to retain only the key phase components in the Fourier spectrum. This results in ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 713.64pt, "improved defect shape resolution, removes the requirement for pre-identifying defect-free areas ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 727.44pt, "within the field of view, and enables the inspection of high thermal conductivity materials [1]. ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 741.24pt, "In practice, the pixelwise discrete Fourier transform is applied to the discrete thermograms ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 755.04pt, "recorded by an infrared (IR) camera over a defined interval (from 0 to N) (1). Consequently, ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 769.08pt, "we obtain amplitude and phase sequences as a function of frequency ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(409.8pt, 769.08pt, "𝑓")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.76pt, 769.08pt, " instead of time-series ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#44546a"))
  #line-at(71.042pt, 532.08pt, "Figure 1: The following schematic representation provides a visual flowchart of a standard thermographic testing, data")
  #line-at(71.042pt, 542.399pt, "acquisition, and processing workflow. he left tree of the figure illustrates the methodical evaluation process that employs known")
  #line-at(71.042pt, 552.721pt, "defects, whether artificial or integrated, with fully known parameters such as size, position, depth, and material, which are ")
  #line-at(71.042pt, 563.158pt, "evaluated using suitable metrics (e.g., SNR, Tanimoto). The right branch represents the real-world application without prior ")
  #line-at(71.042pt, 573.6pt, "knowledge of the existence or location of a defect or the most representative image within the sequence. ")
]

