  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.8pt, 616.8pt, "A variety of established methods exist for evaluating the spatial homogeneity of image data. ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 630.6pt, "These approaches typically rely on subdividing an image into macroscopic subregions ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 644.4pt, "(macropixels), from which local statistical descriptors are derived. Several homogeneity criteria ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 658.2pt, "have been proposed in the literature, including macropixel-based indices originally developed ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 672pt, "for assessing blending processes in solid particulate systems [17], and distribution-based ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 685.8pt, "methods such as the Distributional Homogeneity Index (DHI), which compares local ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 699.6pt, "macropixel statistics with the global intensity distribution [19]. Further studies have extended ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 713.4pt, "these concepts by adjusting subregions sizes or introducing alternative measures to quantify ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 727.2pt, "distribution similarity [35, 36]. In addition to hyperspectral imaging approaches, the theoretical ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 741pt, "foundations of quantitative homogeneity assessment are also grounded in classical mixing ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 754.8pt, "theory. Mixing indices developed for solid particles and pharmaceutical blends [18, 20-22] ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 768.6pt, "describe the transition from ordered to statistically mixed states based on spatial distributions, ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
