  #line-at(70.8pt, 95.52pt, "A further method of evaluating the validity of the proposed methodology and the resulting ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 109.32pt, "findings is to compare these with theoretical predictions. To this end, a one-dimensional ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 123.12pt, "analytical solution of an N-layer theoretical model [16] was employed as a reference ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 136.92pt, "framework. Previous studies have shown good agreement between this analytical model and ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 150.72pt, "experimental infrared thermography (IRT) measurements, supporting its suitability for ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 164.52pt, "comparison with experimental results [8, 9, 43]. Furthermore, numerical simulations are used ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 178.32pt, "to estimate the thermal diffusion length [44], with particular focus on the difference between ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 192.12pt, "reference (1-layer model) and defect regions (3-layer model). The resulting contrast curves can ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 205.92pt, "be interpreted as indicators of defect detectability as a function of frequency. For instance, when ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 219.72pt, "considering the frequency-dependent ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(260.76pt, 219.72pt, "HI")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(273.48pt, 219.72pt, " curves obtained from amplitude images of PPT ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 233.52pt, "experiments for six different defect regions and comparing them with the corresponding ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 247.32pt, "simulations, namely the contrast curves based on the thermal diffusion length, several ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 261.12pt, "qualitative correlations become apparent (Figure 13). First, it can be observed that the contrast ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 274.92pt, "peaks for all ROIs are located very close to each other in both diagrams and occur in the vicinity ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 288.72pt, "of 0.1 Hz. Furthermore, the width of the peak region becomes progressively narrower, and the ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 302.52pt, "slope of the curves becomes steeper from ROI 1 to ROI 6, as illustrated with a slightly yellow ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 316.32pt, "arrow in Figure 13. Another notable observation concerns the frequency at which the difference ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 330.12pt, "in thermal diffusion length becomes zero, which is found to be very similar for all ROIs. The ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 343.92pt, "corresponding frequency, or frequency range, may be interpreted as a “blind frequency” region, ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 357.72pt, "in which defect detectability is not expected. A similar behavior is observed in the experimental ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 371.52pt, "data, where the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(149.64pt, 371.52pt, "HI")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(162.24pt, 371.52pt, " curves show a comparable transition at lower frequencies, marked as the ")
  #line-at(70.8pt, 385.32pt, "“knee point” in Figure 13. This phenomenon can be explained by the fact that the ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(498.48pt, 385.32pt, "HI")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
